      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this leaves the region pointing at a page the host gets back. reg-\u003epriv is set before anything that can fail, and now that handle_lpi_enable is in the path the function can return an error, but the unwind in pkvm_init_redist_emulation only donates the page back and leaves rd_reg-\u003epriv pointing at it. The host side then frees it, so it goes back to the page allocator with the region still referencing it.\n\npkvm_handle_rdist_emulation only tests region-\u003epriv for NULL, so the next trapped access to that redistributor takes the post-init handlers and reads redist-\u003ebase out of host memory. redist_ctlr_write ends in writel_relaxed(value, redist-\u003ebase + GICR_CTLR) and redist_ctlr_read returns what it read to the host, so that is an EL2 write and an EL2 read with the address chosen by the host.\n\nIt looks reachable without much effort: with GICR_TYPER.CommonLPIAff 0, redist_lpi_aff returns 0 for every redistributor, so they are all in one group and handle_lpi_enable returns -EFAULT unless every redistributor with LPIs enabled has an identical GICR_PROPBASER. Pointing PROPBASER or PENDBASER somewhere that makes the share or the pin fail would do it too.\n\nCould we assign reg-\u003epriv last, the way pkvm_init_gic_its_emulation does it, or clear it on the error path? Separately, pkvm_drop_host_privileges discards the result of gic_redist_deprivilege, so none of this is visible when it happens. The ITS side threads its error out through its_end_deprivilege, and it would be good to match that.",
